# Dataset fixtures must keep LF line endings everywhere — bxp-cli
# emits LF on every platform when generating *.csvx output, so a
# CRLF-converted *.expected on a Windows checkout (default
# core.autocrlf=true) would always diff against the freshly produced
# *.csvx and break scripts/test.sh's regression loop.
datasets/**/*.csv     text eol=lf
datasets/**/*.csvx    text eol=lf
datasets/**/*.expected text eol=lf
datasets/**/*.json    text eol=lf
datasets/**/*.json5   text eol=lf

# The expression corpus is fed line-by-line to `bxp-fmt --expr`
# (scripts/test-06-expr-corpus.sh), which rejects a stray CR as
# `UnexpectedChar '\r'`. On a Windows checkout (default core.autocrlf=true)
# a CRLF-converted corpus would fail every line — same rationale as the
# dataset fixtures above. Keep it LF on every platform so the planned
# CI-hardening flutter/test.sh run is green on the Windows runner too.
scripts/test-06-expr-corpus.txt text eol=lf

# Flutter regenerates these plugin registrants on every `flutter pub get`
# / build with LF on every host. Without an explicit eol they fall under
# the Windows default core.autocrlf=true, which flags a permanent phantom
# CRLF diff (the file is committed LF, but git wants to convert it to CRLF
# on the next touch), so they show as modified on every Windows checkout
# even though nothing changed. Pin them to LF so the working tree matches
# the committed blob and the churn stops.
**/generated_plugin_registrant.cc   text eol=lf
**/generated_plugin_registrant.h    text eol=lf
**/GeneratedPluginRegistrant.swift  text eol=lf

# Every page under docs/reference/ is generated (scripts/gen-docs.sh's
# GENERATED_PAGES), and `--check` — the "docs catalog drift" step in
# scripts/test-04-desktop.sh — byte-diffs the committed page against a freshly
# generated one. Both generators (tools/zig-doc-gen, tools/dart-doc-gen) write
# LF on every host, so on a Windows checkout (default core.autocrlf=true) the
# CRLF-converted committed page differs from the generated one on every single
# line and the gate reports a whole-file drift that is nothing but line endings.
# Same rationale as the dataset fixtures and the expression corpus above.
docs/reference/*.md text eol=lf
